<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The subject land — about 12 acres at Nilambur Amsom, Edakkara village, Malappuram — had been held through a lessee chain since a 1960 lease. When the Forest Department obstructed cultivation in 1988, the holders applied to the Forest Tribunal under Section 8 of the Kerala Private Forests (Vesting and Assignment) Act, 1971, contending the land never vested because it was not a private forest on 10 May 1971, the appointed day. The Tribunal agreed in 2001; a Division Bench of the Kerala High Court affirmed in 2008 (MFA No. 166 of 2002); and the State&rsquo;s civil appeal, filed in 2010, reached judgment before a Bench of Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Pamidighantam Sri Narasimha and Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Alok Aradhe on 16 November 2026.</p>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Vesting is automatic — but definitional.</strong> Section 3(1) vests private forests in the State on the appointed day without any further act, but only land answering the statutory definition vests. Everything therefore turns on the character of the land on 10 May 1971.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Burden and standard.</strong> The claimant bears the burden of showing the land was not a private forest, on a preponderance of probabilities — a burden the respondents discharged before the Tribunal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Concurrent findings respected.</strong> The findings were held neither perverse nor otherwise open to interference; the appeal failed on that threshold alone.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>New evidence refused.</strong> The 1900 notification came too late, with no due diligence shown — and it did not identify the land anyway. The application was dismissed with the appeal.</div>
</div>

<h2>The appellate lesson, beyond forest law</h2>
<p>Few provisions are invoked as hopefully, and rejected as routinely, as the plea to reopen facts in a final court. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s civil appellate practice — whether the case arrives by certificate or through special leave under Article 136 — draws a consistent line: questions of law travel upward freely; concurrent findings of fact stay below unless the challenger demonstrates perversity, which means a finding no reasonable forum could have reached on the record. Parties planning litigation strategy should internalise the consequence: the trial forum is where the factual war is won or lost, and an appellate brief that merely re-argues the evidence is a brief against the architecture of the system.</p>
<p>The new-evidence ruling carries its own discipline. Public records do not excuse late production; a party relying on an old notification must show both diligence and relevance — here the State showed neither, since the document neither surfaced with explanation nor connected itself to the survey numbers in dispute.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Front-load the factual record: survey reports, revenue entries, cultivation evidence and inspection notes belong before the first forum, not in an interlocutory application fifteen years later.</p>
  <p>Frame appellate grounds as questions of law — misconstruction of the statutory definition, wrong burden allocation — because re-appreciation pleas will be turned away at the threshold.</p>
  <p>When seeking to adduce additional evidence on appeal, address due diligence expressly and tie the document to the land, transaction or issue with precision.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judgment for general information. Land vesting questions are intensely record-specific and outcomes vary with the evidence in each claim.</div>
